From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:15:19 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gateway: a name a TUN's fake-ip answers is fetched, not
 refused
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

specialNets held 198.18.0.0/15, RFC 2544's benchmarking range, among the
addresses no caller's URL is downloaded from. With Clash's TUN mode in
fake-ip (or Surge's enhanced mode) the resolver answers every name with
an address in that range and carries the connection through the tunnel
to the real host, so the judged resolution always landed there and
web_fetch refused every page, example.com included (#2593). The provider
icons have let the range through for the same reason since #252: no
machine on the user's network sits in it, so a fetch aimed at it reaches
the internet host the name stands for where a tunnel answers, and nowhere
at all where none does. The range is let through here too, and the rest
of the guard holds as ea3dee96 left it: loopback, RFC 1918, fc00::/7,
link-local with the cloud metadata service, CGNAT, documentation,
reserved, and every other special range still refuse a caller's URL,
every resolved address is still checked before the request is pinned to
it, the address dialled is checked again, and each redirect goes through
the same.

Docs: docs/subsystems/gateway-routing.md (the guard paragraph names the
exemption, web_fetch's list its new test).

Tests written first, each red before the range came out of specialNets,
green after: TestPublicAddr names the range's four corners public and,
just beside it, a documentation address private;
TestWebFetchReadsANameAtAFakeIPAddress reads a page whose name resolves
to 198.18.5.1, a local server standing in for the tunnel's proxy since
no tunnel answers in the test — the proxy is sent the fake address the
name was pinned to, never the name; its red form said the page "is at a
private address ... which web_fetch doesn't read", the report's words.

Fixes #2593
