-- A URL magpie downloads but didn't choose itself is downloaded from public addresses only, wherever the request came from ([`image_fetch_guard.go`](../../internal/gateway/image_fetch_guard.go)). That is an image a caller names (an `/v1/images/edits` or `/v1/images/generations` reference image, for an images API that takes files, Gemini's own API or Code Assist: `fetchPicture` in [`draw.go`](../../internal/gateway/draw.go), `publicClient`), a page `web_fetch` reads (`webFetchClient`), and an image or video a vendor answers by URL, which what the model was asked to draw or do can steer (the inline download for a `b64_json` caller in `draw.go` and `/v1/videos/{id}/content` in [`video.go`](../../internal/gateway/video.go), `vendorClient`; generate_image's own download in [`internal/imagemcp`](../../internal/imagemcp/imagemcp.go), `PublicClient`). Every address the host resolves to must be public (`publicAddr`: not loopback, RFC 1918, fc00::/7, link-local with 169.254.169.254, CGNAT 100.64/10, unspecified, multicast, documentation, reserved, Teredo, nor an IPv4-mapped, NAT64 or 6to4 form of one), and the request goes to the address checked, so a name that resolves elsewhere the second time changes nothing; a direct connection is checked again on the address dialled (`guardDialer`). Through a proxy the proxy is sent that address, never the name (an `https://` CONNECT or SOCKS keeps the name for TLS and Host; a plain `http://` URL through an HTTP proxy is asked for by the address in Host too). Each redirect, up to 5, is checked the same, and a caller's image must sniff as an image. A request from this computer gets nothing more: a reverse proxy or relay in front of magpie on the same host makes every request it carries arrive from loopback, so where a request came from is never a reason to reach a private address. Two things are: settings.json's `fetchPrivate` (`settings.FetchPrivate`, none by default, no Settings page, this computer's own and never brought by a sync or restore), a list of names, `name:port`s, IPs and CIDR ranges the user lets magpie download from (`allowFor`; a settings.json that can't be read lets nothing extra in), and for a vendor's URL the `host:port` of that provider's own endpoints, which the user named themselves (a provider on the LAN that serves its images from itself). A vendor's image that can't be downloaded is answered by its URL, never dropped; its video is a 502 naming why. A URL left for the vendor to fetch (a chat model's message) is the vendor's to fetch.
+- A URL magpie downloads but didn't choose itself is downloaded from public addresses only, wherever the request came from ([`image_fetch_guard.go`](../../internal/gateway/image_fetch_guard.go)). That is an image a caller names (an `/v1/images/edits` or `/v1/images/generations` reference image, for an images API that takes files, Gemini's own API or Code Assist: `fetchPicture` in [`draw.go`](../../internal/gateway/draw.go), `publicClient`), a page `web_fetch` reads (`webFetchClient`), and an image or video a vendor answers by URL, which what the model was asked to draw or do can steer (the inline download for a `b64_json` caller in `draw.go` and `/v1/videos/{id}/content` in [`video.go`](../../internal/gateway/video.go), `vendorClient`; generate_image's own download in [`internal/imagemcp`](../../internal/imagemcp/imagemcp.go), `PublicClient`). Every address the host resolves to must be public (`publicAddr`: not loopback, RFC 1918, fc00::/7, link-local with 169.254.169.254, CGNAT 100.64/10, unspecified, multicast, documentation, reserved, Teredo, nor an IPv4-mapped, NAT64 or 6to4 form of one; 198.18.0.0/15, RFC 2544's benchmarking range, is let through, since Clash's TUN mode with fake-ip and Surge's enhanced mode answer every name with an address there and carry the connection to the real host through the tunnel (#252, #2593)), and the request goes to the address checked, so a name that resolves elsewhere the second time changes nothing; a direct connection is checked again on the address dialled (`guardDialer`). Through a proxy the proxy is sent that address, never the name (an `https://` CONNECT or SOCKS keeps the name for TLS and Host; a plain `http://` URL through an HTTP proxy is asked for by the address in Host too). Each redirect, up to 5, is checked the same, and a caller's image must sniff as an image. A request from this computer gets nothing more: a reverse proxy or relay in front of magpie on the same host makes every request it carries arrive from loop